$b['Ornamental moth and exact same ornamental moth'] = 'Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: both tattoo designs alone on a plain white background, side by side, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so they fill most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of two real people together, each showing their own tattoo exactly where described, healed tattoos on real skin in soft even light, the rest of their bodies modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed areas bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoos in both panels are identical. The tattoos: Person A: Ornamental moth. Person B: Exact same ornamental moth. Same art, completely different placement. Mysterious, elegant, intimate. In the right panel are two adults, two women (best friends). Person A (woman) wears tattoo A - placement: behind left ear. Person B (woman) wears tattoo B - placement: inner right forearm. Create exceptional contemporary matching tattoo artwork designed for TWO PEOPLE. Matching Tattoos is not automatically a romantic category. The relationship may be: Guy and girl Girl and girl Guy and guy Partners Best friends Brothers Sisters Siblings Parent and adult child Family Chosen family Or any other meaningful connection Do not assume romance. Do not assume heterosexual couples. Do not assume matching people need matching bodies. Do not assume matching tattoos need matching placements. Do not assume matching tattoos need identical artwork. Do not assume matching tattoos need to be small. Do not assume matching tattoos need to be cute. Do not make every design sentimental. Do not make every design about love. Allow: Beautiful Dark Macabre Gothic Funny Strange Surreal Sexy Minimal Massive Colorful Black and grey Fine Line Traditional Neo-Traditional Realistic Illustrative Japanese-inspired Geometric Abstract Experimental The two tattoos can relate through: Identical artwork Mirrored artwork Shared visual language Different scale Different treatment Complementary subjects Opposing subjects One shared scene Different perspectives One artwork divided between two people Physical connection Shared negative space A third image created when the people meet Every tattoo must be a genuinely desirable piece of tattoo artwork. Do not create novelty clip art merely because the idea is funny. Do not create simplistic symbols unless simplicity genuinely makes the concept stronger. Private jokes should feel personal rather than childish. Dark concepts should be allowed to become genuinely dark. Matching tattoos may involve: Skulls Skeletons Anatomical hearts Ravens Snakes Demons Angels Ghosts Vampires Moths Fire Storms Predators Dark fantasy Gothic architecture Death imagery Horror imagery Surreal transformations Do not sanitize the category into friendship bracelets. At the same time, do not make everything dark. The collection should move naturally between beautiful, fun, intimate, strange, adventurous, serious, emotional and dark work. ARTISTIC AMBITION IS MORE IMPORTANT THAN NOVELTY. DUAL BODY PLACEMENT RULE EVERY CONCEPT HAS TWO INDEPENDENT WEARERS. PERSON A and PERSON B must each receive an explicit body placement. Do not automatically duplicate the placement. Examples: PERSON A, finger PERSON B, wrist PERSON A, behind ear PERSON B, inner forearm PERSON A, ankle PERSON B, shoulder PERSON A, rib PERSON B, calf PERSON A, thigh PERSON B, upper arm PERSON A, collarbone PERSON B, wrist PERSON A, chest PERSON B, shoulder blade Use the same body placement only when it improves the concept. For physically connecting tattoos, coordinate: Placement Scale Orientation Viewing angle Distance between bodies so the artwork actually connects when the people are positioned together. For independent matching or complementary tattoos, choose the strongest placement for EACH INDIVIDUAL PERSON. A large person and small person do not need the same tattoo scale. A heavily tattooed person and first-time tattoo wearer may require completely different placement decisions. One person may want visible placement. The other may want private placement. DESIGN FOR TWO INDIVIDUAL HUMAN BODIES. PEOPLE VARIATION Across the complete collection naturally rotate: Guy and girl Girl and girl Guy and guy Also include: Friends Partners Brothers Sisters Siblings Mother and adult daughter Father and adult son Mother and adult son Father and adult daughter Grandparent and adult grandchild Chosen family Vary naturally: Skin tone Age within the adult range Body build Height Existing tattoo coverage Fashion Presentation Do not make the two people look like matching models. THE ARTWORK CONNECTS THEM. PRESENTATION FORMAT Generate ONE finished presentation image for every matching concept. LEFT SIDE: Show BOTH tattoo artworks cleanly on a neutral background. If they are identical: Show the artwork clearly enough to establish that both people receive the same design. If mirrored: Show both mirrored pieces. If complementary: Show both complete artworks beside one another. If split: Show both halves positioned so the viewer understands the completed artwork. If different scale: Show both versions at their intended relative sizes. If the designs create a third image: Show the relationship clearly. RIGHT SIDE: SHOW TWO REAL PEOPLE. Both people must be visible simultaneously. Person A wears Tattoo A on Person As specified body placement. Person B wears Tattoo B on Person Bs specified body placement. The tattoos must match the LEFT artwork exactly. Do not invent different tattoo designs for the body panel. Do not automatically put both tattoos on forearms. Do not automatically use the same body part. Respect the specific Person A and Person B placements. CONNECTING TATTOO PRESENTATION When the artwork physically connects, position the two people so the viewer can immediately understand the interaction. Examples: Two wrists beside one another Two forearms touching Two shoulders meeting Two calves aligned Two hands approaching Two backs beside one another The connection must actually work geometrically. Do not fake a connection that would be impossible on real bodies. INDEPENDENT MATCHING PRESENTATION When the tattoos do NOT physically connect, allow the two people to stand naturally. They do not need to touch. The viewer simply needs to see both tattoos clearly enough to understand their relationship. One tattoo may be: On a finger while the other is: On a wrist. One may be: Behind an ear while the other is: On an arm. One may be: On a thigh while the other is: On a shoulder. THAT IS INTENTIONAL'
